IMPACT STATEMENT CARD
Latent Probing of Toxicity Detection


INTENDED USE

This project explores the use of linear probes on hidden representations
from a frozen language model to detect potentially toxic user prompts.

The intended use is research into AI safety, trustworthiness, and
mechanistic interpretability.

The approach may serve as an additional component of a broader safety
or moderation system.


POTENTIAL BENEFITS

A toxicity probe may enable earlier identification of potentially
harmful inputs.

Potential benefits include lightweight toxicity detection, improved
understanding of where toxicity-related information emerges within
model representations, and the ability to add an additional safety
signal without retraining the underlying language model.


POTENTIAL RISKS

The system may produce false positives, incorrectly classifying benign
content as toxic.

It may also produce false negatives, allowing toxic content to pass
undetected.

Biases in the source datasets may be reflected in the trained probe.
Performance may vary across languages, dialects, demographic groups,
writing styles, and contexts.

A binary classification system may also oversimplify context-dependent
forms of harmful language.


FAIRNESS AND BIAS

Multiple datasets were used to provide variation in the training data.
However, combining datasets does not eliminate their underlying biases.

Differences in annotation practices and dataset composition may affect
model behavior.

Particular attention should be given to false positives involving
identity-related terminology and to performance on groups or language
varieties that are underrepresented in the training data.


PRIVACY

The project uses publicly available research datasets and does not
collect new personal data.

The system is not intended to identify individuals or infer sensitive
personal characteristics.


TRANSPARENCY AND ACCOUNTABILITY

The underlying Gemma model is frozen and a logistic regression probe
is trained on its hidden representations.

The use of a linear probe provides a relatively simple classification
layer, although the underlying model representations remain complex.

The probe should not be treated as an authoritative determination that
a particular piece of text is harmful.


LIMITATIONS

Competition performance does not guarantee equivalent real-world
performance.

The training data is primarily English-language toxicity data and may
not generalize equally to other languages or domains.

The binary classification format does not represent toxicity severity,
target, context, or type.

The probe should therefore be treated as a research prototype and
should not be used as the sole decision-maker in high-stakes moderation
or enforcement systems.
